def section(a,b,text):
 global s
 start=s.index(a)+len(a);end=s.index(b,start)
 s=s[:start]+'\n\n'+text.strip()+'\n\n'+s[end:]
def change(a,b):
 global s
 assert a in s,a[:100]
 s=s.replace(a,b,1)
section('## Abstract','Keywords:', '''Separately calibrated weekly and 28-day rainfall forecasts can disagree when added. Temporal reconciliation restores agreement, but its predictive value depends on the information it combines. We examine climate information, aggregation structure and forecast combination using ECMWF hindcasts for 2000–2019. Nested year-block validation excludes adjacent years from training. Four primary Australian domains are followed by four geographical extensions and a northern ACCESS-S2 check. We compare direct aggregate calibration with calibrated weekly summation, then combine coherent forecast distributions using constant, seasonal or state-dependent weights. None of the original primary comparisons passes multiplicity adjustment. The northern ECMWF climate effect is exploratory, concentrated in summer and a few years, and not clearly reproduced by ACCESS-S2. Raw ECMWF outperforms the individual calibrated alternatives in the northern forward evaluation. State-dependent mixing reduces the continuous ranked probability score by 1.22% of raw forecast score relative to climate-informed weekly summation (95% interval 0.33–2.03%), but does not improve on constant mixing. Covariance diagnostics overpredict reconciliation gains in every primary domain-season case, while additional geographical climate contrasts are mixed. Climate information can therefore benefit a calibrated forecast without establishing an additional reconciliation benefit. Simple coherent combinations remain competitive, and the tested state indicators do not reliably identify when greater complexity is useful.''')
change('Their usefulness for rainfall depends on the forecasts being combined.', 'Their value for rainfall depends on the forecasts being combined.')
change('These studies motivate a controlled comparison of forecast information, aggregation structure and dependence, rather than an expectation that coherence will improve every score.', 'These studies motivate a controlled comparison of forecast information, aggregation structure and dependence.')
change('A further question follows when the raw ensemble or weekly aggregation performs better than reconciliation: can information available at forecast initialisation identify the more useful distribution?', 'The changing ranking of these forecasts raises a further question about combination. Information available at initialisation, termed the forecast state here, might help determine how much weight to give each distribution.')
change('Here we test a narrower question by mixing complete coherent rainfall trajectories and comparing state-dependent weights with constant weights and simple inner-score selection. This makes forecast combination a controlled extension, rather than treating a flexible mixture as a new reconciliation theory.', 'We mix complete coherent rainfall trajectories and test whether weights that respond to this state outperform constant weights. Selection by held-out training scores provides another simple alternative.')
change('The study contributes a separation of climate-information and reconciliation effects, an evaluation of forecast selection and combination, and transfer checks across geographical supports and forecast systems.', 'The study separates climate information from reconciliation, tests forecast selection and combination, and examines transfer to additional locations and a second forecast system.')
change('They are geographical supports rather than official climate regions.', 'These fixed boxes define the areas over which rainfall is averaged; they are not official climate regions.')
change('The six nonnegative parameters are fitted by minimising the closed-form continuous ranked probability score (CRPS).', 'The six nonnegative parameters minimise the continuous ranked probability score (CRPS), a distributional forecast loss expressed in rainfall units.')
change('Let b contain the four weekly amounts. The five-node hierarchy contains the weeks and their total; the seven-node hierarchy also includes the first and second fortnights:', 'Let b contain the four weekly amounts. Each node denotes one forecast accumulation. The five-node hierarchy contains the weeks and their total; the seven-node hierarchy also includes the two fortnights:')
change('A historical rank template then assigns those quantiles to joint trajectories.', 'A historical rank template assigns those quantiles to joint trajectories, each representing one possible sequence of weekly and aggregate amounts.')
change('Potential CRPS is uncertainty minus resolution in this empirical decomposition. These components describe how the distributional score changes; identifying the responsible physical processes requires further evidence.', 'Reliability measures calibration error; potential CRPS is uncertainty minus resolution, describing the remaining score after that error is removed. This statistical decomposition does not identify a physical mechanism.')
change('The same weights apply to all nodes of a trajectory, so the mixture remains coherent. This operation selects a whole trajectory from a component distribution; it does not average independently sampled weekly amounts. Constant, seasonal and state-dependent softmax models use two coefficient vectors, with the H1 logit fixed at zero for identification.', 'Mixing assigns probability to whole trajectories and preserves their sums. A softmax transformation exponentiates fitted linear scores and normalises them to positive weights summing to one. Constant, seasonal and state-dependent models use two coefficient vectors; fixing the H1 score at zero removes an arbitrary common offset.')
change('The raw ensemble itself has zero aggregation discrepancy. Equal component weights provide a further control.', 'Equal component weights provide a further control.')
change('For decision diagnostics, training-only terciles define ranked probability skill scores, while fixed 50 and 100 mm totals define Brier scores and idealised cost–loss curves. These are generic rainfall thresholds, not crop-specific loss models (Supplementary Tables S19–S21).', 'Decision diagnostics use training-only terciles for ranked probability skill scores and fixed 50 and 100 mm totals for Brier scores. Idealised cost–loss curves compare the expense of protective action with loss from an unprotected rainfall event (Supplementary Tables S19–S21).')
change('The mixture experiment provides a direct test of whether forecast state helps choose among the competing information pathways.', 'The mixture experiment tests whether the forecast state helps weight the competing distributions.')
change('Forecast combination can hedge the differences between coherent distributions without requiring a reliable conditional ranking of them. A climate coefficient or a changing mixture weight is therefore insufficient evidence of a useful meteorological decision rule. These results do not support progressing to selected circulation composites as if a stable regime rule had already been established.', 'A constant mixture spreads probability across the coherent alternatives without estimating a changing ranking. The tested state variables do not improve that ranking reliably enough to support a meteorological selection rule. Their fitted coefficients therefore describe this combination model, not a demonstrated circulation mechanism.')
section('## 6 Conclusions','## Data availability', '''Climate information, temporal reconciliation and forecast combination contribute differently to subseasonal rainfall prediction. None of the three primary domain-averaged comparisons passes multiplicity adjustment. The northern ECMWF climate effect is exploratory and concentrated in summer and a few years. Climate-informed weekly calibration captures much of the same benefit, so the improvement over rainfall-only weekly summation does not establish an additional reconciliation advantage.

Forward evaluation changes the practical comparison. In northern Australia during 2015–2019, raw ECMWF outperforms the individual calibrated alternatives, and climate-informed weekly summation outperforms aggregate reconciliation. Raw ECMWF also leads over those years under cross-validation. Calibration gains within a statistical system must therefore be distinguished from gains over the dynamical forecast.

The geographical and second-system checks qualify the northern finding. The western Kimberley domain retains a favourable aggregate comparison, but incremental climate effects are mixed elsewhere and uncertain in ACCESS-S2. Covariance diagnostics also overpredict reconciliation gains in all sixteen primary domain-season cases. Neither geographical proximity nor estimated error covariance reliably identifies where aggregate climate information will add skill in these experiments.

Combining coherent distributions offers a simpler response to their changing performance. Constant mixtures remain competitive across the primary domains and in the northern ACCESS-S2 check. State-dependent weights improve on climate-informed weekly summation, but not on constant mixing, and perform less well in the northern forward evaluation. The resulting practical sequence is to test calibration against the raw ensemble, climate predictors within weekly forecasts, and direct aggregates against their coherent sum. Additional complexity earns its place through held-out forecast skill and decision value; temporal consistency alone supplies an accounting constraint, not a guarantee of either.''')
